import{_ as t,c as a,o as n,ag as r}from"./chunks/framework.ARG558ZT.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Data structures and representation","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"cs-foundations/data-structures.md","filePath":"cs-foundations/data-structures.md"}'),i={name:"cs-foundations/data-structures.md"};function s(o,e,d,l,c,h){return n(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="data-structures-and-representation" tabindex="-1">Data structures and representation <a class="header-anchor" href="#data-structures-and-representation" aria-label="Permalink to “Data structures and representation”">​</a></h1><p>A data structure determines which operations are cheap and which invariants the program must maintain. Choose from the <strong>required operations and ownership</strong>, rather than replacing every array with a Map.</p><h2 id="representation-and-selection" tabindex="-1">Representation and selection <a class="header-anchor" href="#representation-and-selection" aria-label="Permalink to “Representation and selection”">​</a></h2><p>The costs below use conventional abstract implementations and constant-cost element/key operations. JavaScript specifies observable semantics, not universal engine time bounds. An application-owned dense array is different from a sparse array with getters or proxies.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Structure</th><th>Useful operations / model costs</th><th>Tradeoffs and invariant</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dense dynamic array</td><td>Index O(1); search O(n); end append amortized O(1) in a geometric-resize model</td><td>Contiguous logical indices; middle insert/remove may move O(n) elements; bounds and mutation matter</td></tr><tr><td>Singly linked list</td><td>Insert/remove at head O(1); find/index O(n); append O(1) with a maintained tail</td><td>Node/link overhead; removing after a <strong>known predecessor</strong> O(1), finding it is not free</td></tr><tr><td>Doubly linked list</td><td>Insert/remove a known node O(1)</td><td>Two links per node; must maintain both directions/head/tail; searching still O(n)</td></tr><tr><td>Stack</td><td>Push/pop top O(1), or amortized in a resizing-array model</td><td>LIFO; undo state, parser nesting, DFS frames</td></tr><tr><td>Queue</td><td>Enqueue/dequeue O(1) with fixed ring or suitable linked representation</td><td>FIFO; fair admission/BFS; overflow and empty behavior explicit</td></tr><tr><td>Deque</td><td>Both ends O(1) in a conventional ring/block implementation</td><td>More involved index/resize ownership; arbitrary middle access/removal is a different operation</td></tr><tr><td>Map / Set</td><td>Expected O(1) under usual hash model; ECMAScript guarantees average sublinear access</td><td>Identity/equality, retained references, iteration order and invalidation; no universal worst-case O(1)</td></tr><tr><td>Binary search tree</td><td>Lookup/insert O(h)</td><td>Left/right key ordering; unbalanced h may be n; balanced-tree guarantees require balancing</td></tr><tr><td>Binary min-heap</td><td>Peek O(1), push/pop O(log n), bottom-up construction O(n) in the conventional model</td><td>Parent no greater than children; <strong>not fully sorted</strong>, arbitrary lookup O(n)</td></tr><tr><td>Graph adjacency list</td><td>O(V+E) storage, enumerate neighbors O(degree)</td><td>Vertex identity/directedness/edge policy; traversal needs visited state on cycles</td></tr></tbody></table><p>For ordinary records with known fields, object properties are appropriate. For dictionary keys use Map or a deliberate null-prototype object with <code>Object.hasOwn</code>, not inherited property lookup. <code>get</code> returning undefined does not distinguish a missing Map entry from a stored undefined value. The <a href="./../senior_growth/General_ES6/What_is_ES6_Map_What_is_the_difference_between_Map_17be8106c24d808caa13f8ae4a80115c.html#equality-ownership-ordering-and-serialization">existing collection lesson</a> demonstrates object identity, NaN/zero, serialization and WeakMap lifetime.</p><h2 id="hash-lookup-and-collision-assumptions" tabindex="-1">Hash lookup and collision assumptions <a class="header-anchor" href="#hash-lookup-and-collision-assumptions" aria-label="Permalink to “Hash lookup and collision assumptions”">​</a></h2><p>A conventional hash table maps a key to a bucket, then resolves collisions by comparing keys within that bucket or probing other slots. Equality must still be checked: equal hash values do not prove equal keys. Controlled load factor and an appropriate hash model can give expected constant-time access; many collisions can make an operation linear in the number of entries. Resizing rebuilds placement and has a separate amortized cost. Long string hashing/equality also depends on key length.</p><p>This explains the model used in cost estimates; ECMAScript Map/Set can use other mechanisms and does not expose buckets or require that implementation. Their equality/iteration semantics are the public contract.</p><h2 id="stacks-queues-and-linked-nodes" tabindex="-1">Stacks, queues and linked nodes <a class="header-anchor" href="#stacks-queues-and-linked-nodes" aria-label="Permalink to “Stacks, queues and linked nodes”">​</a></h2><p>An array with end push/pop is a simple stack. Repeatedly using <code>shift()</code> as a queue may move/reindex the remaining elements; do not base a general O(V+E) BFS argument on it. For a one-shot traversal a growing array plus head index is simpler, but keeps consumed entries until return.</p><p>A linked queue owns a head and tail. Empty means both null; nonempty means the tail&#39;s next is null. Enqueue links after tail; dequeue advances head and resets tail when removing the last node. Losing that reset can disconnect future entries. Public node references complicate ownership: removing a foreign/stale node is not automatically safe. A general linked-list implementation is optional here; the reference uses a bounded ring instead.</p><h2 id="worked-ring-wraparound" tabindex="-1">Worked ring wraparound <a class="header-anchor" href="#worked-ring-wraparound" aria-label="Permalink to “Worked ring wraparound”">​</a></h2><p>The <a href="./../workbench/cs-foundations/collections.html#fixed-capacity-fifo">fixed-capacity FIFO source</a> stores <code>head</code> and <code>size</code>:</p><ul><li>Valid state: <code>0 ≤ size ≤ capacity</code>, <code>0 ≤ head &lt; capacity</code>.</li><li>Logical item <code>i</code> occupies <code>(head + i) % capacity</code>, for <code>0 ≤ i &lt; size</code>.</li><li>Enqueue writes at <code>(head + size) % capacity</code>, then increments size.</li><li>Dequeue reads at head, clears that slot, advances head modulo capacity and decrements size.</li></ul><p>With capacity 3: enqueue A/B/C, dequeue A, enqueue D. The physical slots become <code>[D, B, C]</code>, while logical order is B/C/D. Physical order is not the contract. These transitions preserve the logical sequence, proving FIFO behavior. Clearing a consumed slot releases that reference; the owner still retains the dequeued object if it stores it elsewhere.</p><p>Construction allocates O(capacity) slots. Each valid enqueue/dequeue is worst-case O(1) <strong>in this fixed-array model</strong>, with no resizing. Full enqueue and empty dequeue throw; storing undefined remains supported because size, rather than a sentinel value, defines occupancy. Capacity is an application policy, not a global memory or concurrency limit.</p><h2 id="trees-and-graphs-are-different-ownership-models" tabindex="-1">Trees and graphs are different ownership models <a class="header-anchor" href="#trees-and-graphs-are-different-ownership-models" aria-label="Permalink to “Trees and graphs are different ownership models”">​</a></h2><p>A proper rooted tree is connected, acyclic and gives each nonroot node one parent. A graph can have multiple paths, cycles, self-loops or disconnected vertices. A shared child means a DAG, not that tree representation. A binary tree has at most two child slots; a binary <strong>search</strong> tree also satisfies key ordering. A binary heap has another, weaker ordering invariant.</p><p>Use <a href="./trees-graphs-traversal.html">tree/graph traversal</a> for representation, correctness and space analysis. A scheduler selects the next minimum priority with a <a href="./priority-queues.html">heap</a>; it does not need an entire sorted list after every insertion.</p><p><a href="./../practice/cs-foundations/collections-and-queues.html">Separate queue/cache exercise</a> · <a href="./sorting.html">Next: sorting</a></p>',21)])])}const m=t(i,[["render",s]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
